# AttaLambda @VERSION@ — @TARGET_NAME@

This is the self-contained AttaLambda release archive for the target named
above. `BUILD-MANIFEST.txt` identifies its exact source commit.
It contains the `attalambda` command, its private Racket CS 9.3 runtime,
and five example programs. You do not need to install Racket, run `raco`, or
register a package.

## Download, verify, and extract

Obtain both `@ARCHIVE_NAME@` and its sibling `SHA256SUMS` from the same
AttaLambda GitHub Release, and keep them in the same directory. Before
extracting the archive, verify that its bytes match the supplied checksum:

```@COMMAND_LANGUAGE@
@VERIFY_COMMAND@
```

If verification succeeds, extract the archive and enter its root directory:

```@COMMAND_LANGUAGE@
@EXTRACT_COMMAND@
@ENTER_COMMAND@
```

## Run the first program

Confirm the packaged version, then run the included hello program:

```@COMMAND_LANGUAGE@
@EXECUTABLE@ --version
@EXECUTABLE@ examples@PATH_SEPARATOR@hello.attl
```

The output is:

```text
AttaLambda @VERSION@
Hello from AttaLambda.
```

## Write an AttaLambda program

AttaLambda source is UTF-8 text with the lowercase `.attl` extension. The
first line must be exactly `#lang attalambda`. The language currently uses
Racket-style Lisp parentheses; no Racket installation is involved at run
time. Numbers are exact rationals (`Rat`): integer and fraction literals
such as `3`, `-7/3`, and `1/2` are ordinary values, arithmetic never
rounds, and inexact literals like `1.5` are rejected at load time.

Save this as `my-program.attl` in the extracted archive root:

```racket
#lang attalambda

(stdout "My first AttaLambda program.\n")
```

Run it with:

```@COMMAND_LANGUAGE@
@EXECUTABLE@ my-program.attl
```

## Command results and exit statuses

`attalambda FILE.attl` writes the program's requested stdout bytes directly.
The command itself prints no representation of the final lambda value.

| Status | Meaning |
| ---: | --- |
| `0` | The source loaded and completed normally without calling exit, including when it produced an AttaLambda Error or Result Err; or the program explicitly called `(exit 0)`. |
| `1` | The program explicitly called `(exit 1)` to report unsuccessful completion. |
| `64` | The command arguments were invalid. |
| `65` | The filename, `#lang` declaration, UTF-8 source, or AttaLambda syntax was invalid. |
| `66` | The source was missing, unreadable, a symbolic link, not a regular file, or refused by the dotenv-file rule. |
| `70` | The launcher encountered an unexpected internal failure. |

Diagnostics use the `AttaLambda:` prefix on standard error. Run
`@EXECUTABLE@ --help` for the complete command grammar.

`exit` accepts only Rat 0 or 1. Another type returns TypeMismatch Error; any
other Rat returns InvalidCount Error, without terminating. An incoming Error
bubbles normally. Pure AttaLambda decides whether a condition is fatal and
chooses the status; the host only performs that explicit request. Neither
Error nor Result Err is automatically printed or translated into process
failure. A performed exit does not return, so later effects do not run; an
exit in an unselected branch remains unforced.

## Optional static checking

Check the program without running it:

```@COMMAND_LANGUAGE@
@EXECUTABLE@ --check my-program.attl
```

The checker reports exact coverage and inferred types, using the same existing
source syntax without annotations. It reads one source snapshot and never
executes the program, consumes its answers, performs its file/network/output or
exit effects, or accesses shell history. Other command flags cannot accompany
`--check`. Ordinary execution and runtime type checks remain unchanged.

| Check status | Meaning |
| ---: | --- |
| 0 | FULL PASS: every source obligation is established and the report was written and flushed. |
| 1 | FAIL: at least one supported type constraint conflicts. |
| 2 | PARTIAL: no definite conflict, but some obligations remain unproved. |
| 64 | Invalid arguments. |
| 65 | Invalid source. |
| 66 | Source unavailable or refused by the existing path policy. |
| 70 | Internal analyzer or report-delivery failure. |
| 130 | Checking was interrupted. Previously written report bytes may remain. |

Definitions and expressions have separate exact counts, including unused bodies
and unselected branches. Generated syntax does not inflate coverage. Zero
denominators display `n/a`; an empty source explicitly passes vacuously. A
rounded 100.0% can still be PARTIAL. Incomplete definitions have a reason or
dependency path, never a verified success-only hint. Locations use one-based
lines and zero-based columns. A static FAIL does not mean execution must crash.

Identity infers `forall a. a -> a`, while `double` defined with `add` infers
`Rat -> Rat`. Definitions and source lets support rank-1 polymorphism; lambda
parameters and recursive self calls stay monomorphic. Lists and branch results
have homogeneous static types. Variables marked `:data` exclude functions and
Error from canonical container data; Error remains a separate type.

This first version leaves `unwrap-ok` partial even behind `is-ok`, and `head`
partial even on a visible nonempty List. It has no variant, nonempty, or numeric
range refinements. Raw host protocols and unsupported data domains also remain
unproved through aliases and callers. `div` returns Result(Rat), so division by
zero is represented rather than a type conflict. Supported I/O wrappers retain
their Result shapes without performing effects during checking.

FULL PASS relies on trusted built-in, recursive-lowering, and host/codec
contracts. It does not verify their implementations, prove termination,
guarantee external operation success or resource lifetimes, exclude deliberate
Error/Result Err values, or protect future unchecked callers. It adds no
runtime tags or guards and is not a formal-verification claim.

Running afterward is a separate invocation that reads the file again. A check
applies to the captured snapshot, not subsequent edits; checking and running
are not an atomic operation.

## Interactive shell and program input

Start `@EXECUTABLE@` with terminal stdin and stderr for the `atta> ` prompt.
Enter expressions without `#lang attalambda`; for example, `(add 2 3)` prints
`=> 5`, and `TRUE` prints `=> TRUE`. Definitions stay lazy and silent.
Use `@EXECUTABLE@ --repl` for redirected transcripts, or add `--no-history`
to disable persistent source history. The two flags may appear in either order;
they cannot be combined with a source filename.

| Command | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `:help` | Show syntax and commands. |
| `:names` | List committed user definitions without forcing their values. |
| `:load "path.attl"` | Run a standalone file and publish its definitions after success. |
| `:echo on` / `:echo off` | Enable or disable automatic expression-result display. |
| `:reset` | Discard definitions and close resources; retain echo and history settings. |
| `:quit` | End the shell. |

Redefinition affects later entries. Existing closures and delayed values keep
the bindings they captured. A loaded file sees the public language, not shell
definitions; loading it again reruns it. Bare file expressions are not echoed.
Each source buffer is checked completely before execution. Failed evaluation
does not publish new names, but effects already performed cannot be undone.

Use `:echo off` before demanding raw functions, including inside containers:
their rendering behavior is unspecified. Echo-off does not delay program stdout.
`(read-line UNIT)` reads a byte-preserving line only when demanded and returns
`OK(SOME("answer"))` or `OK(NONE)` at EOF. A saved read is shared; a fresh call
inside a function reads again. Wait for a program's prompt before pasting its
answer. Text already accepted as an editor buffer is source, and cannot also
be interpreted as program input. Source and program reads share the original
input stream, and program answers never enter source history.

Ctrl+C cancels a pending entry or running demand and returns to a fresh
interactive prompt. Ctrl+D at a fresh prompt exits. The editor restores terminal
state and falls back to a plain interactive collector if it cannot initialize.
History retains at most 1,000 submitted source entries, with a 1 MiB persisted
file under Racket's preference directory (normally
`~/.config/racket/attalambda/history-v1` on Linux). Unsafe or damaged history
is ignored. `--no-history` keeps in-memory navigation without persistent access.
Personal Racket/editor initialization files are not run.

Transcripts have no unsolicited prompts, banner, terminal controls, or persistent
history access. Normal completion is 0; a recovered reader/expansion/command/native
failure makes final transcript status 1. Unfinished source at EOF is 65, and
transcript Ctrl+C is 130. Explicit `(exit 0)` or `(exit 1)` overrides that status.
Ordinary language Error/Result Err values do not count as native failures.
Interactive EOF or `:quit` after recovered errors is 0.

## Small Lisp sugar

Version 0.7.0 adds four source conveniences. This program prints
`[5, 6]small` without a trailing newline:

```racket
#lang attalambda
(def sum = (lambda (x y) (add x y)))
(print (list (sum 2 3) (let ((x 2) (y (add x 4))) y)))
(stdout (cond ((lt 2 3) "small") (else "large")))
```

`list` expands to existing typed `cons` and `NIL`; `(list)` is `NIL`.
Lambdas accept one or more identifiers and curry into unary lambdas, so partial
application works. Multi-binding `let` nests sequentially: later bindings can
use earlier ones, and `(let () body)` returns the body. Existing unary lambda
and `(let name = value body)` forms remain valid. `cond` requires a final
`else` and exactly one condition and result per preceding clause. It uses the
existing strict typed `if` and leaves unselected branches unforced. No runtime
value, operation, representation, or host capability is added.

## Authority and safety

AttaLambda does not sandbox programs. A program runs with the launching
process's authority:

- `stdout` can write to the terminal or redirected output.
- `read-line` can consume standard-input bytes when a program demands it.
- `read-file` can read files that the process can access.
- `write-file` can create, truncate, or replace files that the process can
  access; AttaLambda does not prompt or make backups.
- TCP operations can connect to reachable hosts and listen on permitted local
  ports.
- `exit` can terminate the program's own process with status 0 or 1.

Inspect and trust an `.attl` program before running it. Run unfamiliar programs
from a directory and operating-system account whose files and network access
you are willing to expose.

## Release notes

Version 0.9.0 adds optional `--check FILE.attl` static checking without running
the program. It reports full passes, definite conflicts and partial results,
with exact coverage, inferred signatures and source locations. Existing syntax,
runtime type checks, ordinary execution and the interactive shell are preserved.
The checking section above describes the supported types and deliberate limits.

Version 0.8.0 combines program line input with the `atta>` shell.
It preserves the existing language, lazy effects, lexical binding snapshots,
standalone source validation, and absolute object-language purity. The shell
adds only private tooling for source entry, terminal editing, and session state.

Version 0.6.0 adds pure value-to-String conversion and generic `print`.
`value-to-string` renders tagged values, including nested Lists, Options,
Results, Maps, and Errors. The eleven type-specific functions use the same
display rules. For example, `(print (cons 1 (cons TRUE NIL)))` writes
`[1, TRUE]`. `print` returns stdout's Result and adds no newline; use
`(stdout "\n")` for a line separator. `stdout` continues to emit String bytes
directly, while `(print "hello")` includes the display quotes: `"hello"`.

String display escapes non-ASCII bytes separately, and Maps retain their stored
order. Display is not a serialization format. Passing a raw function directly
or inside data has unspecified behavior. Rendering remains pure unary lambda
calculus; printing uses the existing stdout capability.

Version 0.5.0 adds `(rec name argument ... = body)` for pure self recursion.
Change a self-recursive declaration from `def` to `rec`; ordinary `def`
must now be acyclic. Direct or indirect recursive module bindings are rejected
with status 65 and a specific source diagnostic. Mutual top-level cycles
remain invalid even when their declarations use `rec`. Acyclic forward
references, partial application, lexical shadowing, and lazy evaluation remain
supported. `rec` expands through a private lambda-calculus fixed-point term;
it adds no runtime primitive or host capability.

This self-contained distribution supports Linux x86-64; it provides the direct
`attalambda FILE.attl` command, the canonical `.attl` and `#lang attalambda`
source contract, explicit exit 0/1, and stdout, whole-file, TCP, and minimal
HTTP examples backed by the single language-visible host boundary. Artifacts
produced for other targets are internal portability evidence, not supported public downloads.

## Known limitations

- `BUILD-MANIFEST.txt` records the target, toolchain, and observed native-library
  assumptions. It does not record a tested operating-system version or establish
  a minimum compatibility guarantee.
- There is no installer, automatic PATH configuration, program-argument
  API, package manager, updater, formatter, debugger, or editor integration.
- Programs receive no sandbox, permission prompt, backup, timeout, or
  concurrency layer.
- The networking surface is blocking TCP plus the documented minimal
  HTTP/1.1 subset. It does not provide TLS, UDP, asynchronous serving, or a
  general HTTP framework.
- The archive has no AttaLambda cryptographic signature.
- Linux x86-64 is the only supported public binary target. No other operating
  system, processor architecture, compatibility floor, or long-term support
  schedule is promised.

